[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I cannot reproduce a black hole at the top router. End-to-end connectivity between the hosts is healthy: pc_0_0 -> pc_0_1 and pc_0_1 -> pc_0_0 both succeed with 0% loss and ~1.1–1.2 ms RTT. On the implicated device (super_spine_router_0), control and data planes look normal. BGP to both spines is established (neighbors 172.16.0.1 AS 65100 and 172.16.0.3 AS 65101 are up, each advertising two prefixes). The super-spine has valid routes for both 10.0.0.0/24 and 10.0.1.0/24, selected via next-hop 172.16.0.3 on eth1, and its interface counters show no errors or drops on eth0/eth1. This routing choice is a normal BGP best-path selection, not a drop policy. 

Mechanism: because the super-spine’s RIB and FIB contain valid paths and its links are error-free, packets forwarded across the top are routed and delivered; a black hole would show loss on the host pings and/or drops/error counters, which we do not see. Localization: super_spine_router_0, bgpd and interfaces eth0/eth1, all healthy. 

Root cause: No fault found on the super-spine; the reported “black hole” condition is not present and was likely a transient or host/application-side issue. 

Corrective fix: No restart is needed. If the problem recurs, capture timestamps and a packet trace on pc_0_0 and at leaf/ spine uplinks, and run traceroute during the event to localize. Consider enabling ECMP if load-balancing across both spines is desired, but current behavior is correct. 
=== END ===